{
  "dom_id": "step-q77510",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/f2e79d1a16c7bba471bed8a443673449c7398486d19a84e12a91ca514c793329.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $p$ and $q$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $-2p-4q?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n-2p-4q=-2p+(-4q).\n$$\n\n- The vector $-2p$ has the direction that is opposite to $p$ and $2$ times the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-4q$ has the direction that is opposite to $q$ and $4$ times the magnitude.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/de82fa1937ae9f7ccb1b739cc8e6ea9848a71ea216df600917706717711aa081.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-4q$ starting from the head of $-2p.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n-2p-4q\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $-2p$ to the head of $-4q.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/2d388be7761bd2fbae0593b8e715f1873e4170e577132b4a6838412e3629c480.png)",
